From 8b38f7396e7da3f7b5bfbc39ef100a5200d032e0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slydlake <sly.dlake@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:50:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/16] Add qwen35_mxfp4: Qwen3.5/3.6/3.8 dense with OCP-MXFP4
 weights, W4A8 on libr4d

A compressed-tensors mxfp4-pack-quantized release (llm-compressor GPTQ/RTN)
converts and declares end to end, and is served at 4-bit weights x E4M3
activations (mxfp4a8).

- core/format: quant_method compressed-tensors + format mxfp4-pack-quantized
  is the descriptor 'mxfp4'; each X.weight_packed / X.weight_scale pair is
  aliased as X.weight (fp4_e2m1 [N][K]) plus its e8m0 companion, a TRIVIAL
  fp4_e2m1*e8m0[1x32] weight that rad-convert copies byte for byte
  (llm-compressor packs low nibble first, as a 4-bit plane does here).
- arch/common/rad_fp8.h: LinearFP8 serves an MXFP4 weight at mxfp4a8 against
  the same E4M3 activation pair as every fp8 linear. The MLP's gated fold is
  an fp8a8 tile and is skipped for it.
- arch/qwen35_mxfp4: qwen35_fp8.cpp under the descriptor 'mxfp4'
  (the qwen35moe_fp8 pattern).
- libr4d, three MXFP4 x E4M3 GEMM rows reading the activation scale per row
  or per (row, 128 of K), the fp8 family's grid:
  * gemm_mxfp4a8_nt_m64 (skinny row, existing) learns both grids.
  * gemm_mxfp4a8_decode (M <= 64, priority 55): split-K across workgroups
    with a fused last-arriver reduction, every thread fencing its partial
    before the counter. Ported from SlyBase/vllm-sly-radiance
    (radiance_mxfp4_fp8_gemm_decode). Split = smallest filling the card;
    BK 128 (64 when K % 128); ks/bk/nt are tunables. The one-fragment form
    (M <= 16) prefetches the next K slab into registers: gate_up 171.3 ->
    165.2 us (573 GB/s; card read roof 600-620), +1.2 % on story/code/explain.
  * gemm_mxfp4a8_tiled (large M): 256 x (32*TN) block staging A through LDS
    once per K slab, fragment-order weight unpacked once per slab, ported
    from the same project. Activation scales for the (row, 128) grid go
    through LDS ratios, not registers: TN=4 192 -> 183 VGPRs, no scratch,
    occupancy 8 (it was 5 with scratch). tn {0,2,4}, 0 = TN 4 from
    M >= 2048. Priority R4D_PRIO_GEMM_TILED, the skinny row keeps M <= 64.
- tools/opshapes.h: a shape recipe for gemm_nt_q dtype mxfp4a8, so rad-tune
  reaches both MXFP4 rows. (rad-tune times weights that fit the 64 MB
  Infinity Cache, so its pick for small decode shapes can lose in the model.)
- r4d_selftest: mxfp4a8_numeric against a host decode of the planes, both
  grids, zero-scale blocks, ragged M/N, exponent spread into the subnormal
  tail, every split/slab of the decode row.

Measured on an R9700 (gfx1201, ROCm 7.2.4) with a GPTQ-MXFP4 Qwen3.8-27B
(288 MXFP4 linears, 16.94 GiB): healthy in 12 s, MTP depth 3, answers
correctly; M=8 verify down 97 -> 88 us, ssm_inz 104 -> 81, qg 78 -> 67,
o/out 61 -> 41; decode story/explain/code 44/77/111 -> 48/85/121 tok/s;
prefill 1355 -> ~2300 tok/s (3.6k-28.8k prompts).

The decode row's split-K partial lives in the op scratch, not in a per-stream
workspace, and the row is concurrent:

With MXFP4 drafter linears (below) the first replay of a recorded
draft pass was refused: "draft pass 0 at 8 tokens issued differently from its
recording ... its arguments differ at byte 42" -- the split-partial pointer,
which came from a workspace looked up by stream, while the engine records a
draft pass on one stream and plays it on another.

The engine path now takes the partial from the op scratch (a scratch hook
sizes it at the band's widest M over the split rule at every M of the band; a
short scratch runs unsplit), which is fixed in the recording. The arrival
counters come from a small device block keyed on the output buffer, as
gemm_fp8a8_nt_m16's split_ctr does: two launches writing one output are
ordered by that dependency, two that may overlap write different outputs. The
standalone entry keeps the per-stream workspace.

Verified: selftest 35/35; served for 2000+ decode steps and four concurrent
streams without a refusal. The MXFP4 drafter over 12 mixed prompts x 256
tokens: 3.17 tok/step (fp8 drafter 3.20), 33.9 ms/step (34.9), 93.5 tok/s
(91.6).

Since the split partial is the op scratch (tracked read+write by the core)
and the arrival counters are keyed on the output, as gemm_fp8a8_nt_m16 keys
its own, the row no longer needs its stream to order it. Drop it from
kStateful, fix the stale comments, so the engine may overlap decode GEMMs on
two streams.

Selftest: three split decode GEMMs (ks 4/2/4) launched in pairs on two
streams with disjoint scratch slices must be byte-identical to their serial
runs, and a serial launch afterwards must still be (counters left at zero).
The mxfp4a8 decode numeric cases now get their op scratch, and the overlap
case syncs before comparing and reports what differs.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
