From c884ad57480c2c1f5d9489dc307678b6c35fa3d2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slydlake <sly.dlake@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 14:50:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/16] qwen35_mxfp4: q|gate, k and v as one projection; the
 prologue feeds the gate

An OCP-MXFP4 checkpoint has its scale group along K, so q_proj (q and gate),
k_proj and v_proj concatenate along rows into one attn_qkv weight over the
same normed activation: one decode GEMM where k and v were two launches of
their own at ~2.6 MB each, almost all fixed cost (AttnGatedFP8::fuse_qkv). The
container decides: one holding a separate attn_k is served as before; a
conversion fuses. The fused weight has no KV row span, so it refuses a
replicated-KV world (world > n_head_kv_all) at declare.

The gate consumers read [q|gate] as rows a head at a pitch of 2*head_dim,
which a column view of the fused row cannot give. First, [q|gate] was copied
out with one bf16 row copy; k and v go into the fused prologue as column
views (it takes a row pitch on both and writes k to its own plane). Then the
fused qk_norm_rope_gate(_kv_store) prologue reads q|gate as a column view of
w.qkv and stores the gate contiguously through its gate_out operand into
w.qg, and gate_quant_fp8 reads it from there. The q|gate row copy (op_cqg) is
issued only where it is still needed: unfused steps, the merge-fused sparse
attention (reads the interleaved slice) and bf16 models.

Bit-identical to the copy: gate_out is a bf16 element copy of the same bytes,
the prologue's q arithmetic reads the same values at another pitch, and the
gate quantiser's arithmetic does not depend on its gate pitch. One launch and
2*T*q_dim bytes less per full-attention layer.

Measured (R9700, 210 W, DFlash2 k7, 12 mixed prompts x 256 tokens, twice):
step 33.82-33.94 -> 33.60-33.71 ms; 17x23 and the Paris prompt unchanged;
2000 decode steps and four concurrent streams without a refusal.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
